      "writtenOn": "2026-05-08T20:49:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In `ft_get_time_local`, the code formats a string into a local array, and then uses `snprintf` to append milliseconds into a second array.\n\n**Current Code:**\n\n```c\n\tstrftime(buff, sizeof(buff), \"%m-%d %H:%M:%S\", \u0026timeinfo);\n\tsnprintf(time_buff, sizeof(buff), \"%s.%03ld\", buff, tv.tv_usec / 1000);\n\n```\n\n**Feedback:** You can do this in-place. `strftime` returns the number of characters written. You can use that return value to append the milliseconds directly to the final buffer, eliminating the need for the intermediate `buff` completely.\n\n**Suggested Rewrite:**\n\n```c\nstatic void ft_get_time_local(char *time_buff, size_t max_len)\n{\n\tstruct timeval tv;\n\tstruct tm timeinfo;\n\n\tgettimeofday(\u0026tv, NULL);\n\tlocaltime_r(\u0026tv.tv_sec, \u0026timeinfo);\n\n\tsize_t len \u003d strftime(time_buff, max_len, \"%m-%d %H:%M:%S\", \u0026timeinfo);\n\tif (len \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 len \u003c max_len) {\n\t\tsnprintf(time_buff + len, max_len - len, \".%03ld\", tv.tv_usec / 1000);\n\t}\n}\n\n```",
      "range": {
