      "message": "In several places, the code declares a struct and then uses `memset` to zero it out. Modern, idiomatic C allows you to zero-initialize a struct at the point of declaration using `\u003d {0};`. This removes an extra function call and cuts down on boilerplate.\n\n**Current Code (`ft_sg_io` / `ft_device_disable` / `usb_write`):**\n\n```c\n\tstruct sg_io_hdr io_hdr;\n\t// ... variable declarations ...\n\tmemset(\u0026io_hdr, 0, sizeof(io_hdr));\n\n\tstruct bulk_cb_wrap cbw;\n\tmemset(\u0026cbw, 0, sizeof(cbw));\n    \n\tfocal_usb_device_port_method_t method;\n\tmemset(\u0026method, 0, sizeof(focal_usb_device_port_method_t));\n\n```\n\n**Suggested Rewrite:**\n\n```c\n\tstruct sg_io_hdr io_hdr \u003d {0};\n\tstruct bulk_cb_wrap cbw \u003d {0};\n\tfocal_usb_device_port_method_t method \u003d {0};\n\n```\n\n*(Note: You already do this correctly for arrays like `unsigned char transfer_cmd[FT_CMD_LENGTH] \u003d { 0 };`. Apply the same pattern to structs.)*",
