      "message": "The `main` function copies `argv` strings into a local `tmp` buffer just to use `strncmp`. This is a common anti-pattern. Command-line arguments in `argv` are guaranteed to be null-terminated, so copying them provides no additional safety and only clutters the code.\n\n**Current Code:**\n\n```c\nmemset(tmp, 0, sizeof(tmp));\nstrncpy(tmp, argv[2], sizeof(tmp) - 1);\ntmp[PATH_MAX - 1] \u003d \u0027\\0\u0027;\n\nif (!strncmp(tmp, FT_ENTER_FORCE_UPDATE_CMD_STR, strlen(FT_ENTER_FORCE_UPDATE_CMD_STR))) { ... }\n\n```\n\n**Suggested Rewrite:** You can discard the `tmp` buffer entirely and use `strcmp` directly against `argv`. This cuts about 15 lines of buffer management out of `main`.\n\n```c\nif (!strcmp(argv[2], FT_ENTER_FORCE_UPDATE_CMD_STR)) {\n\tFF_LOGD(\"cmd:%s\", FT_ENTER_FORCE_UPDATE_CMD_STR);\n\tinfo.next_mode \u003d FORCE_UPDATE_MODE;\n} else if (!strcmp(argv[2], FT_ENTER_SET_FLASH_WP_CMD_STR)) {\n// ...\n\n```",
